From bc9eab8aa9fa772fceb4dcecda7eefd250176d8f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:27:40 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make the recommendation nudge fail-safe: warn only on a
 positive in-gate signal

Two exact-head reviews of PR1220 found the boundary denylist fail-open: any ask
it did not recognize got the "apply it yourself" nudge (17/17 missed in
t_01a1ce72aa3e4cb0, 39/52 after widening in t_01a1ce964f2521ad). The space of
David-only asks is open-ended, so a denylist cannot be the gate.

recommended_choice_warning now warns only when the action text or the
recommended choice itself carries a code/scope signal (_RECOMMENDATION_IN_GATE:
PR, commit, test, fixture, module, helper, shim, rename, refactor, timeout,
retry/backoff, config, flag, pre/postmerge, lint, :port, worktree, function,
code, docs, schema, regex, parser, branch but not branch protection). Scope
alone never qualifies, since workers write it as boilerplate. The boundary and
named-person patterns stay as a global veto and gain the reviewers' category
terms (buy/order/book/paid/renew/cancel/terminate/decommission/restore/prod,
devices and doors, terms/agree/sign, share/reply/submit, deploy/release,
enable/disable, failing checks, compute and cloud nouns), plus @handles,
possessive names and lowercase "ask david".

Tests: all 53 recorded reviewer asks plus plan-review mixed cases stay quiet
with both a neutral and an in-gate scope; neither-list, scope-only and
substring-trap asks stay quiet; eight in-gate controls still warn. 84 of the
new tests fail on 3c35529fa2. Held-out probe written after the design: 0/27
David-only asks nudged (1/27 before the failing-checks term), 12/12 in-gate
asks still nudged; the old head nudged 16/27. Acceptance (a)-(e) unchanged.
